{
  "id": "green-chemistry-mixture-purification",
  "title": "Purifying a Mixture with Green Chemistry",
  "description": "Design and obtain approval for a selective thermal-decomposition method with no fixed source recipe, using configured sample mass, tare convention, heating/cooling limits, and replicate count; acquire same-balance constant-mass evidence, calculate composition from recorded measurements, recover unheated mixture and heated product separately, and review one teacher-assigned report.",
  "audience": "Chemistry students preparing for or reflecting on Mixture Purification",
  "learningGoals": [
    "Design and justify a teacher-approved gravimetric method; the source does not prescribe a fixed recipe, so sample mass, tare convention, heating/cooling limits, and replicate count remain configured.",
    "Use lid-askew heating, tongs, cooling, and one balance to establish constant mass safely.",
    "Determine mixture composition from the balanced decomposition stoichiometry and recorded mass loss.",
    "Evaluate scientific quality, reporting quality, atom economy, and another green-chemistry principle in a teacher-provided report."
  ],
  "safetyNotes": [
    "Execution remains locked until a teacher approves the proposed method and safety precautions.",
    "Never ignite a burner under a fully covered crucible; keep the lid askew with a visible vent gap.",
    "Move a hot or warm crucible with crucible tongs and cool it at the teacher-configured heat-safe location.",
    "Never place a hot crucible on a balance; heat can damage the instrument and convection can bias the reading.",
    "Use the same balance for the empty, loaded, and every cooled-cycle mass.",
    "Follow teacher-configured baseline PPE and local safety rules.",
    "Keep unheated mixture and heated product in separately labeled recovery containers."
  ],
